    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into the Alcmaeonid family through his mother, Pericles rose with the democratic party, saw his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> ostracised in 461 BCE, and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> until he died in 429 BCE.</li><li>He paid citizens for jury service, restricted citizenship to children of two Athenian parents, and drew on the treasury of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> to fund the temples of the Acropolis, a use of the allies' money one modern scholar has called one of the largest embezzlements in history.</li><li>His defensive strategy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, and his part in bringing the war about, have divided historians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> to Donald Kagan and Victor Davis Hanson.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Contemporary comedians mocked the unusually large size of Pericles's skull, calling him &quot;Squill-head&quot; after the sea-onion; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> claimed this was why he was always shown in a helmet, but the helmet was the symbol of his rank as general.</p></aside>
    </section>

