      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 495 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the son of Xanthippus and of Agariste of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">472 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Pays for the staging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s The Persians at the Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysia">Dionysia</a>, a sign of his wealth</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">461 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After Ephialtes's reform of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>, secures the ostracism of his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">451 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Proposes the law limiting Athenian citizenship to those of Athenian parentage on both sides</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">449 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Proposes a decree allowing 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> to be used for rebuilding the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">440 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Leads the expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, which capitulates after a siege of eight months</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">431 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> invades, brings the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> within the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and that winter delivers the Funeral Oration</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">429 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Re-elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> a year after being deposed and fined, he dies of the plague, which had killed both his legitimate sons</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pericles was born around 495 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the son of the politician Xanthippus and of Agariste, a member of the powerful and controversial noble family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a>. Xanthippus was ostracised in 485–484 BCE but returned to command the Athenian contingent at the Greek victory at Mycale five years later. Agariste was the great-granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>, tyrant of Sicyon, and the niece of the Athenian reformer Cleisthenes, and her family's connections helped to start her son's political career. He belonged to the Attic phyle of Acamantis.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Agariste dreamed a few nights before his birth that she had borne a lion. The story may allude to the unusually large size of his skull, a favourite target of the comedians. His early years were quiet: the introverted young man avoided public appearances and gave his time to study, which his family's nobility and wealth allowed. He learned music from the masters of the time, perhaps Damon or Pythocleides, and he is considered the first politician to attribute importance to philosophy. He kept company with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protagoras">Protagoras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-elea">Zeno of Elea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxagoras">Anaxagoras</a>, who became a close friend. His proverbial calm and self-control, and perhaps his manner of thought and speech, are often regarded as products of Anaxagoras's influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the spring of 472 BCE Pericles paid for the staging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s The Persians at the Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysia">Dionysia</a>, a liturgy that showed him to be one of the wealthier men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; Simon Hornblower has argued that the choice of a play recalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>'s victory at Salamis signalled support for Themistocles against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says that Pericles stood first among the Athenians for forty years, which would place his leadership from the early 460s BCE. In 463 BCE he was the leading prosecutor of Cimon, leader of the conservative faction, who was acquitted.</p><p>Around 461 BCE Ephialtes, leader of the democratic party and Pericles's mentor, proposed cutting the powers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Areopagus</a>, the aristocratic council, and the Assembly adopted the proposal without opposition. In the same year Pericles achieved the ostracism of Cimon, accused of betraying the city by aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and the murder of Ephialtes left him free to consolidate his authority. He promoted a populist social policy: free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> seats for the poor, a lower property requirement for the archonship in 458–457 BCE and wages for jurymen soon after 454 BCE. In 444 BCE the new conservative leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> (not the historian) accused him of profligacy over the building plan; Pericles offered to repay the questioned costs himself if the dedications bore his name, and in 442 BCE Thucydides was ostracised for ten years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 442 BCE Pericles was re-elected every year to the generalship, the only office he ever officially held, though his influence made him the de facto ruler of the state. He completed the transformation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> into an Athenian empire. The league's treasury was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 454–453 BCE, revolts in Miletus and Erythrae were put down by 450–449 BCE, and around 447 BCE the Coinage Decree imposed Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage, weights and measures on the allies. From that treasury came the funds for the building plan of the Acropolis, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/propylaea">Propylaea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> and the golden statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> by his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>; in 449 BCE he proposed a decree allowing 9,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> to be spent on the temples.</p><p>His campaigns brought both gains and losses. In 447 BCE he settled Athenian colonists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli peninsula</a>, but after the defeat at Coronea Athens lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> revolted in 446 BCE he crushed the rising: the landowners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> lost their properties, and the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaea">Histiaea</a>, who had killed the crew of an Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">trireme</a>, were uprooted and replaced by 2,000 Athenian settlers. The Thirty Years' Peace of 446–445 BCE gave up most of Athens's gains on the mainland. In 440 BCE he led the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, which surrendered after a siege of eight months, and he founded cleruchies at Andros, Naxos, Thurii and Amphipolis.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pericles was attacked throughout his career. His conservative opponents charged him with squandering public money, and just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> his closest associates were prosecuted: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a> for embezzling gold meant for the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and then for impiety, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxagoras">Anaxagoras</a> for his religious beliefs, and his companion Aspasia of Miletus of corrupting the women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, charges that were probably nothing more than unproven slanders. Aspasia was acquitted after a rare emotional outburst from Pericles; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, Phidias died in prison, though this is debated. Pericles himself was asked to account for his handling of public funds.</p><p>Critics since have judged his measures harshly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> say that Pericles made the Athenians "slothful, garrulous and avaricious" by starting the system of public fees, and Plutarch reports others who blamed him for leading the people into bad habits. The 19th-century historian Constantine Paparrigopoulos held that his populism led to the political turmoil after his death, and the Greek academician Angelos Vlachos called his use of the allies' treasury one of the largest embezzlements in human history. Plutarch suggests that he and the Athenians incited the war, and Karl Julius Beloch believes he brought it on to protect his position at home. Of his strategy, Donald Kagan called it "a form of wishful thinking that failed", and Barry S. Strauss and Josiah Ober judged that as a strategist he was a failure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pericles held that war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was inevitable. He sent help to Corcyra against Corinth and proposed the Megarian decree, which shut Megarian merchants out of the markets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the ports of its empire; George Cawkwell argues that it breached the Thirty Years' Peace. Sparta demanded, among other things, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a>, Pericles included, and in a speech <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> gives him he urged the Athenians not to yield. When Archidamus II invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> in 431 BCE, Pericles had already moved the whole population of the countryside within the walls of Athens, refused battle and sent a fleet of 100 ships to raid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. In the winter of 431–430 BCE he delivered the Funeral Oration for the city's dead.</p><p>In the summer of 430 BCE an epidemic devastated the crowded city. Public anger turned on Pericles: he was deprived of the generalship and fined between 15 and 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleon">Cleon</a> named as prosecutor in the ancient sources. Within a year the Athenians re-elected him, but in 429 BCE the plague killed his sister and his two legitimate sons, Paralus and Xanthippus. The Athenians then made his son by Aspasia, Pericles the Younger, a citizen, though his own law of 451 BCE barred him. Pericles died of the plague later that year. On his deathbed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports, he said that "no living Athenian ever put on mourning because of me". Thucydides judged his death a disaster for Athens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The most visible legacy of Pericles is the art and literature of the Golden Age, much of which survives; the Acropolis, though in ruins, is a symbol of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The years from about 461 to 429 BCE are sometimes called the "Age of Pericles", and it was principally through his efforts that Athens gained its reputation as the educational and cultural centre of the Greek world. He has been lauded as "the ideal type of the perfect statesman in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>", and his Funeral Oration is now synonymous with participatory democracy and civic pride. Victor L. Ehrenberg, by contrast, argues that a basic element of his legacy is an Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> that denied democracy and freedom to all but the ruling state.</p><p>How much of his voice survives is uncertain. Pericles never wrote down his speeches, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> recreated three of them, so scholars such as Ioannis Kakridis and Arnold Gomme have debated how far the words are Thucydides's own. Thucydides, an admirer, wrote that Athens was "in name a democracy but, in fact, governed by its first citizen". His war strategy still divides historians: Hans Delbrück called him one of the greatest statesmen and military leaders in history, Athanasios Platias and Constantinos Koliopoulos argue that Athens lost only when it abandoned his strategy, and others hold that it was too defensive to succeed.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pericles led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> as its foremost orator and general from about 461 to 429 BCE, widening its democracy, turning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> into an empire and funding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> from the allies' treasury. He took Athens into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> and died of the plague in its third year, leaving a record that historians have praised and condemned ever since.</em></p>

